        "heading": "1 INTRODUCTION",
        "text": "Real-world data is generally highly structured, and full with symmetry and patterns. This implies that there exists a lower-dimensional set of ground truth factors that is able to explain a significant part of variation present in real-world data. The goal of disentanglement learning is to recover these factors, so that changes in a single ground truth factor are only reflected in a single latent dimension of a model (see Figure 1 for an example). Such an abstraction allows for more efficient reasoning (Van Steenkiste et al., 2019) and improved interpretability (Higgins et al., 2017a). It further shows positive effects on zero-shot domain adaption (Higgins et al., 2017b) and data efficiency (Duan et al., 2020).\nIf the generative ground-truth factors are known and labeled data is available, one can train a model in a supervised fashion to extract the ground-truth factors. What if the generative factors are unknown, but one still wants to profit from the aforementioned benefits for a downstream task? This can be necessary if the amount of labeled data for the downstream task is limited or training is computationally very expensive. Learning disentangled representations in an unsupervised fashion is generally impossible without the use of some priors (Locatello et al., 2019b). These priors can be present both implicitly in the model architecture and explicitly in the loss function (Tschannen et al., 2018). An example of such a prior present in the loss function is low total correlation between latent variables of a model (Chen et al., 2018; Kim & Mnih, 2018). Reducing the total correlation has been shown to have a positive effect on disentanglement (Locatello et al., 2019b). Unfortunately, as we\nshow in more detail in this work, it is highly dataset-specific by how much total correlation should be reduced to achieve good disentanglement. The optimal hyperparameter setting for one dataset might yield bad results on another dataset. To optimize regularization strength, we need a way to evaluate disentanglement quality.\nSo how can we identify well disentangled representations? The evaluation of representation quality, even given labeled data, is no easy task. Perhaps as an instance of unfortunate nomenclature, the often-used term \u201cground-truth factor\u201d implies the existence of a canonical set of orthogonal factors. However, often there exist multiple equally valid sets of ground truth factors, for example affine transformations of coordinate axes spanning a space, different color representations, or various levels of abstraction for group properties. This poses a problem for supervised disentanglement metrics as they fix the ground truth factors for evaluating a representation, judging the models too harshly if they learned another equally valid representation. Furthermore, acquiring labeled data in a practical setting is usually a costly endeavor. The previously stated reasons hinder the usability of supervised metrics for model selection.\nIn this work, we overcome the above mentioned limitations both for learning and evaluating disentangled representations. Our improvements are based on a rather lucid idea: If a VAE learned a truly disentangled representation, a discriminator network should not be able to distinguish reconstructed samples from samples generated by the decoder from a different latent distribution. Depending on the prior of low total correlation, we use a latent distribution where we sample for each dimension independently, calling it the factorial posterior latent distribution. Quantifying the ease of such separation yields a metric independent of specific choice of ground truth factors and reconstruction quality. We show that the similarity of the two distributions is a necessary condition for disentanglement. Further, we can use this property at training time by introducing an adversarial loss into classical training of VAEs. To achieve this, we introduce a discriminator network into training and use the decoder of the VAE as generator. During training we control the weight of the adversarial loss and correspondingly adjust the capacity of the latent space information bottleneck, inspired by (Burgess et al., 2017). By doing so, we allow the model to increase the complexity of its representation as long as it is able to disentangle.\nThis dynamic training procedure resolves the issue of dataset-specific hyperparameters and allows our approach to reach competitive disentanglement on a diverse set of commonly used datasets without hyperparameter tuning.\nOur contributions are as follows:\n\u2022 We identify a novel unsupervised aspect of disentanglement called PIPE and demonstrate its usefulness in a metric with correlation to supervised disentanglement metrics as well as a downstream task.\n\u2022 We propose an adaptive adversarial training procedure (DAVA) for variational auto-encoders, resolving the common issue of disentanglement performance being largely dependent on dataset-specific regularization strength.\n\u2022 We provide extensive evaluations on different commonly used disentanglement datasets to support our claims."
