        "heading": "3 POSTERIOR INDIFFERENCE PROJECTION EQUIVALENCE",
        "text": "We first introduce the notation of the VAE framework, closely following the notation used by Kim & Mnih (2018). We assume that observations x \u223c D\u0303 are generated by an unknown process based on some independent ground-truth factors. The goal of the encoder is to represent x in a latent vector z \u2208 Rd. We introduce a Gaussian prior p(z) = N (0, I ) with identity covariance matrix on the latent space. The variational posterior for a given observation x is then q\u03b8(z|x) = \u220fd j=1N (zj |\u00b5j(x), \u03c32j (x)), where the encoder with weights \u03b8 outputs mean and variance. The decoder with weights \u03d5 projects from the latent space z back to observation space p\u03d5(x|z). We can now define the distribution q(z). Definition 3.1 (EP). The empirical posterior (EP) distribution q(z) of a VAE is the multivariate distribution of the latent vectors z over the data distribution D\u0303. More formally, q(z) = Ex\u223cD\u0303[q\u03b8(z|x)]. We can reconstruct an observation x \u223c D\u0303 the following way: We sample z \u223c q\u03b8(z|x) and then get the reconstruction x\u0302 \u223c p\u03d5(x|z). We informally call observations generated by this process reconstructed samples and denote them as x\u0302.\nThe decoder is not constrained to only project from q(z) to observation space. We can sample observations from the decoder by using different distributions on z. We define a particularly useful distribution. Definition 3.2 (FP). The factorial posterior (FP) distribution q\u0304(z) of a VAE is a multivariate distribution with diagonal covariance matrix. We define it as the product of the marginal EP distrbution: q\u0304(z) = \u220fd j=1 q(zj). We can use the decoder to project z \u223c q\u0304(z) to observation space x\u0303 \u223c p\u03d5(x|z). We informally call observations created by this process generated samples and denote them as x\u0303.\nWe can now define the data distributions that arise when using the decoder to project images from either the EP or the FP. Definition 3.3 (D\u0303EP , D\u0303FP ). D\u0303EP is generated by the decoder projecting observations from the EP latent distribution q(z), i.e. reconstructed samples. D\u0303EP = Ez\u223cq(z)[p\u03d5(x|z)]. D\u0303FP is generated by the decoder projecting observations from the FP distribution q\u0304(z), i.e. generated samples. D\u0303FP = Ez\u223cq\u0304(z)[p\u03d5(x|z)].\nWe can now define the core concept of this paper. We look at the similarity of two data distributions generated by the decoder, D\u0303EP and D\u0303FP .\nDefinition 3.4 (PIPE). The posterior indifference projection equivalence (PIPE) of a VAE represents the similarity of the data distributions D\u0303EP and D\u0303FP . In other words, PIPE is a measure of the decoder\u2019s indifference to the latent distribution it projects from. PIPE (\u03b8, \u03d5) = \u03c9(Ez\u223cq(z)[p\u03d5(x|z)],Ez\u223cq\u0304(z)[p\u03d5(x|z)], where \u03c9 is a general similarity measure.\nUnsupervised disentanglement learning without any inductive biases is impossible as has been proven by Locatello et al. (2019b). Given a disentangled representation, it is always possible to find a bijective mapping leading to a representation which is entangled. Without knowledge of the ground truth factors, it is impossible to differentiate the two representations. We argue that PIPE is necessary for a disentangled model, even when it is not sufficient. Assume that a model has learned a disentangled representation, but D\u0303EP is not equivalent to D\u0303FP . There are two cases in which this could occur. The first option is that the latent dimensions of the model are not independent. This violates the independence assumption of the ground truth factors, consequently the model cannot be disentangled. The second option is that the model has not learned a representation of the ground truth factors and generates samples which are not represented by the ground truth factors. This model would also not be disentangled. Therefore we conclude that PIPE is a necessary condition for disentanglement.\nIn Figure 2 we present reconstructed and generated samples of two different models to support our chain of reasoning. Generated samples of the entangled model (Figure 2 (b)) look visibly out of distribution compared to the reconstructed samples. Generated samples of the disentangled model (Figure 2 (d)) seem to be equivalent to the distribution of reconstructed samples."
